==English==
[[Image:Julia balcony.jpg|thumb|right|A balcony.]]
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbælkəni/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-balcony.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|it|en}} {{m|it|balcone||balcony, floor-length window}} from {{cog|roa-oit|balcone||scaffold}} from {{etyl|lng|en}} {{m|lng|*balko|*balko, *balkon-|beam|sc=Latn}} from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*balkô||beam}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*bhelg'-||beam, pile, prop}}. Akin to {{cog|goh|balco|balco, balcho|beam}}, {{cog|ang|balca||beam, ridge}}. More at {{l|en|balk}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|balconies}}

# An accessible [[structure]] extending from a [[building]], especially outside a window.
#: {{rfquotek|Shakespeare}}
#: {{rfquotek|The Bible}}
# An accessible structure [[overlook]]ing a stage or the like.
#* {{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}
|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=3|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Sepia Delft tiles surrounded the fireplace, their crudely drawn Biblical scenes in faded cyclamen blending with the pinkish pine, while above them, instead of a mantelshelf, there was an archway high enough to form a '''balcony''' with slender balusters and a tapestry-hung wall behind.}}

